  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather with above normal temperatures \nthrough the first half of the week under high pressure.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nThe ridge of high pressure remains overhead today through the\nfirst half of the upcoming week, keeping winds light, skies\nmostly clear, and temperatures above normal. Highs will be in\nthe low to mid 80s today through Monday and Tuesday for inland\nlocations, while lows each night dip into the low to mid 50s.\nHighs will be slightly cooler along the coast with a sea breeze\ndeveloping each afternoon.\n\nMarine fog could push onshore each overnight, and should this\nhappen visibilities could quickly drop, especially throughout\nthe Coastal Downeast region, through the early morning hours.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A system is likely to move in mid-week, \nbringing another round of wetting rainfall.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure lingers over the region this weekend, as a low \npressure system slides along the east coast early next week. \nGenerally, low pressure east of the Carolinas moves northeast in\nthe Atlantic, east of Nova Scotia. Meanwhile, a cold front\napproaches on Monday and lingers to our west on Tuesday. A \nshortwave diving southeast over the Ontario province will \ndevelop a low pressure system over the Great Lakes region late \nMonday into Tuesday. The low will approach our region Wednesday \nproducing rain over our area late Wednesday through Thursday. A\nwetting rainfall is expected, with the potential for 0.5 to 1 \ninch of rainfall. Ensembles show around a 60% chance of at \nleast 0.5 inches with the greatest probabilities along Downeast\nMaine. Some scattered thunderstorms are also possible with this\nsystem Wednesday night into Thursday.",
